===Etymology===
Compare {{cog|tbq-pro||*s-na|crossbow}}, whence {{cog|duu|tana}}, {{cog|nxq|daqna}}.

{{zh-ref|Unger (1990)}} suggests a derivation from {{och-l|努|to tense; to exert}}, as the crossbow shoots arrows by releasing elastic potential energy stored in its limbs. {{zh-ref|Ferlus (1999)}} cites Gernet's opinion that {{och-l|砮|stone arrow-tip}} had been semantically extended to mean the whole crossbow.

More likely, {{och-l|弩}} and {{cog|tbq-pro||*s-na|crossbow}} were Sinitic and Tibeto-Burman renditions of a {{w|Mainland Southeast Asia linguistic area|Mainland Southeast Asian}} Wanderwort ({{zh-ref|Ratliff, 2010}}<!---; {{zh-ref|Blench, 2014}}, unpublished manuscript, to be removed from here as well as About Chinese/references?--->), possibly of {{der|zh|aav}} origin ({{zh-ref|Norman and Mei, 1976}}; {{zh-ref|Benedict, 1990}}; {{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}). Compare other MSEA lexical items meaning "crossbow":
* {{cog|mkh-pro|*snaʔ}}, whence {{cog|vi|ná}}, {{cog|km|ស្នា}};
* {{cog|hmx-pro|*nhaᴮ}}, whence {{cog|mww|hneev}}, {{cog|ium|hnaav}};
* {{cog|tai-pro|*ʰnwɯəꟲ}}, whence {{cog|th|หน้าไม้}}, {{cog|lo|ໜ້າໄມ້}}, {{cog|khb|ᦐᦱᧉᦺᦙᧉ}}.
Also compare {{cog|map-pro|*panaq||to shoot (an arrow)}} (cf. {{cog|mkh-pro|*paɲʔ}}) whence {{cog|ms|panah}}, {{cog|tl|pana|panà}}, {{cog|haw|pana}}.

In contrast, Pain (2020) tentatively proposes a native Chinese origin for {{och-l|弩|tr= *C.naʔ}}, which would then be borrowed in {{w|Proto-Vietic language|Proto-Vietic}} and thence into other {{w|Austroasiatic languages}} as well as {{w|Tai languages|Tai}}.<ref>{{cite-journal|work= halshs-02956831|last= Pain|first= Frederic|year= 2020|title= “Giao Chỉ” (“Jiāozhǐ 交趾”) as a diffusion center of Chinese diachronic changes: syllabic weight contrast and phonologisation of its phonetic correlates|titleurl= https://shs.hal.science/halshs-02956831/document}}</ref>

